WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 125 for July 8th, 2026.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.280
The universe has been busy, let's get into it.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:15.840
A total solar eclipse will turn day to night across northern Spain next month as the moon fully covers the sun.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.920
The moon will completely cover the sun on August 12th,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.520
briefly turning day into night along a path through northern Spain.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.680
Observers in the zone will see the solar corona and planets that are normally hidden by daylight.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.220
The event is the first total solar eclipse visible from mainland Spain in decades.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.820
Local interest has surged, with rural residents who once watched the night sky alone,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.480
now part of widespread public excitement.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.420
Towns along the path of totality report packed accommodations and organized viewing events.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.360
Enrique Bordalo, who grew up stargazing in the Spanish countryside,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.640
now sees his childhood passion shared by thousands preparing for next month's eclipse.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:05.680
Now, shifting focus outward, New Horizons has emerged from its record hibernation.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:12.180
NASA's New Horizons spacecraft has completed its longest hibernation period of nearly a year,

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.360
and is now transmitting data collected in the distant kuiper belt.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.620
The spacecraft remains in good health after waking and is ready to resume science operations far

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.380
beyond Pluto. Mission teams will begin processing the new observations in the coming days.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:34.360
This marks another step in the extended mission studying the outer solar system's small bodies.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.480
Future data releases will focus on objects encountered during the hibernation window.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.740
New Horizons has returned from nearly a year of hibernation with fresh kuiper belt data

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:51.640
now streaming back to Earth. The spacecraft's instruments recorded conditions in a region where

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.460
sunlight is thousands of times weaker than at Earth. Mission teams are already checking instrument

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:03.940
health before full science operations resume. The data will add to the sparse record of objects beyond

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:11.320
Pluto. What distant body will the next downlink reveal? On a different note, the US Space Force has

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:17.920
added two newer firms to its launch program. The US Space Force has included relativity space and

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.260
impulse space in the national security space launch phase three lane one program.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.500
The move opens military launch opportunities to newer companies beyond traditional providers.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.180
Both firms will now compete for future national security payloads.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.680
The decision reflects an effort to broaden the industrial base for assured access to space.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.180
Next steps include contract negotiations and mission assignments.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:51.500
Indian startup Skyroot Aerospace aims to conduct its first orbital launch attempt as soon as July 12th.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:57.200
The company plans to scale quickly toward monthly launches once initial success is achieved.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.720
The vehicle will carry a small satellite payload on this debut flight.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:07.820
Ground teams are completing final checks ahead of the window. Success would mark India's first

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:14.240
private orbital launch. Italian firm DORBIT has signed a contract to provide multiple launches

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:21.500
for Japanese startup Arc Age Space using its Ion satellite carrier. The agreement covers a series

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:27.380
of missions beginning later this year. Arc Edge plans to deploy small satellites for communications

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:33.860
and technology demonstrations. DORBIT's carrier will handle deployment and initial orbit adjustments.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:42.480
The deal expands European launch services for Asian customers. LHA 120 and 40 appears as a glowing

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.800
shell of debris from a star that exploded in the large Magellanic cloud. 163,000 light years away

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:56.520
in Dorado. The blue outer shell marks material expelled by the supernova while reddish patches show

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.320
ongoing emission from smaller regions. The image was captured from Rio Hurtado, Chile.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.160
Such remnants help astronomers study how heavy elements are distributed into surrounding galaxies.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.720
Additional multi-wave length observations could reveal more about the progenitor star.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.380
The bright galaxy Centaurus A, 11 million light years away,

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.760
continues forming stars rapidly despite an ancient collision that is not immediately obvious

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.620
invisible light. The galaxy ranks as the fifth brightest in the sky and has been observed

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:36.620
extensively by both professionals and amateurs. Its warped disc and active nucleus hint at the merger

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:43.120
that occurred billions of years ago. Radio and infrared data reveal structures left by the event.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.140
Further studies will examine how the collision continues to influence star formation.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:55.000
If the Milky Way sat at the center of one of these forming clusters, the nearest neighboring subcluster

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.600
would lie more than a million light years away, roughly 10 times the distance to Andromeda.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.480
Two massive elliptical galaxies anchor each subcluster like gravitational anchors,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.180
pulling smaller galaxies into orbit while the whole system hurdles through space.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:17.720
When the subclusters slam together, their galaxies pass through one another with relatively few direct

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:24.520
collisions because the spaces between stars are so vast. Instead, the dark matter halos and hot

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:30.900
gas interact, slowing the motion and allowing eventual merger. The entire process can repeat

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:37.080
several times before the final cluster settles. We still do not know exactly how much of the final

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:42.600
cluster's mass ends up locked in the central giant ellipticals versus remaining in the diffuse

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:49.580
intra-cluster light. Astronomers used observations of cosmic explosions to suggest the Milky Way's

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:55.880
true size exceeds previous measurements. The technique relies on light from distant events passing

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:02.080
through galactic material. Revised dimensions would affect models of the galaxy's mass and rotation.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.260
The finding challenges assumptions built from earlier surveys. Additional data from upcoming

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:14.860
telescopes will test the new estimate. NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope, scheduled for launch

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:21.460
in August 2026, is projected to discover around 100,000 exoplanets during its mission.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.360
That total would surpass the combined yield of every previous telescope.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:32.880
The observatory will use wide field imaging and micro-lensing to detect planets across a broad

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:39.560
range of distances. Mission planners anticipate the largest statistical sample of exoplanets yet

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:44.360
assembled. Early data releases after launch will begin validating the prediction.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.760
April Link has successfully contacted its initial satellite after a July 7 launch on a SpaceX ride

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:57.560
share mission. The contact clears the path for a data relay demonstration under an experimental

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:04.060
Federal Communications Commission license. Engineers will now test the satellite's relay capabilities

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:09.700
in orbit. The mission aims to prove low latency data forwarding between spacecraft.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.060
Follow-on tests are planned once the demonstration phase begins. The ninth magnitude main belt

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:22.120
asteroid will be visible all night on July 8 in southeastern Aquila near the border with

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:28.940
Scudum. Opposition places it opposite the sun in Earth's sky, maximizing its brightness for the

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:33.480
year. Observers can track its motion against background stars throughout the night.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:39.460
The asteroid is roughly 150 kilometers across and has been studied for decades.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:45.840
Amateur astronomers are encouraged to attempt imaging or visual observations while it remains

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:52.320
well-placed. Recent Ferris State University graduate Francisco Vasquez has started a role at

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.720
Blue Origin focused on space exploration hardware. The position places him in the company's engineering

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:05.320
teams working on crude vehicles. Vasquez's academic background prepared him for systems integration

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:11.680
and testing tasks. His hiring reflects growing pathways from university programs into commercial

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:17.300
spaceflight. Future updates may highlight project assignments tied to upcoming launches.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:24.360
The cottonwood fire burned more than 150 square miles and affected parts of a Utah ski resort.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.860
Satellite imagery captured the fire's extent and burn scar for damage assessment.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:36.400
Firefighters use the data alongside ground reports to monitor spread. The event highlights how

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:42.420
Earth observing satellites support wildfire response. Additional images will track vegetation

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:49.720
recovery in the affected area. NASA's plankton aerosol, cloud, ocean ecosystem satellite is

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.800
undergoing validation of its science data products through coordinated field campaigns.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:09:00.940
Teams compare satellite measurements with ship and buoy observations to refine ocean color and

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:07.200
atmospheric retrievals. The effort supports studies of marine ecosystems and climate interactions.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:14.100
Early results show good agreement between space and surface data. Further campaigns will expand

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:20.100
geographic coverage. Next time, keep an eye on the first downlink from New Horizons as mission

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:26.740
teams begin full processing of the keywap observations. That covers today's space and science news.

